<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Native splash icon (Android 12+): the laser bolt draws itself in, morphs into a paper
  plane, lights its engine, and holds on the burn until MainActivity's exit animation flies
  it off screen. See drawable/splash_laser_bolt.xml for the pre-12 variant, which stops at
  the drawn bolt because the exit API it would need does not exist there.

  The web version uses stroke-dasharray/dashoffset, which VectorDrawable has no
  equivalent for — trimPathEnd is the Android analogue, so the path below starts at
  trimPathEnd="0" (nothing drawn) and animates to 1 (fully drawn).

  The morph is a pathData animation, which Android can only interpolate between two paths
  that are command-for-command compatible: same count, same types, same order. Both paths
  here are M + 5L + Z (six points), which is what makes the tween legal — editing either
  one to a different number of points silently breaks the morph at runtime rather than at
  build time, so keep the command structure intact.

  Point order is what the morph interpolates, and the two shapes are ordered so the bolt's
  outline unfolds outward into wings rather than crossing over itself mid-tween.

  The plane's mid-edge points (7.5,11) and (16.5,11) exist only to satisfy that six-point
  requirement — the silhouette wants a straight leading edge from nose to wingtip, and a
  four-point shape could not morph from the bolt. They are therefore placed exactly on the
  line between nose (12,2) and each wingtip: at y=11, half way down, x is 12 ∓ 4.5. Nudging
  either off that line puts a visible kink back in the wing, which is what an earlier
  (8,11)/(16,11) pair did.

  Why the engine burn loops: the splash cannot leave until the WebView is ready, and that
  wait is not knowable here — it moves with the device, cold vs warm start, and system load.
  Any fixed-length ending freezes as soon as the wait outlasts it, which is exactly how the
  earlier one-shot version came to look stuck. The burn has no such edge: it fills a 200ms
  wait and a 3s wait equally well, it reads as an engine holding at power rather than as an
  object sitting idle, and the take-off always cuts in on something already moving.

  Timing is front-loaded so an early teardown still lands on a complete image: bolt drawn by
  360ms, plane by 780ms, engine lit by 900ms — which is what values-v31/integers.xml holds
  windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration open for, and what MainActivity waits out before
  launching. Nothing here erases the icon; a late teardown should look intentional.

  Sizing follows the Android 12+ spec for an icon with no icon-background colour: a
  288dp canvas whose artwork stays inside a 192dp circle. The path is authored in the
  same 24x24 viewport as the web SVG, so the 0.55 group scale is what keeps it inside
  that circle — the bolt spans 18x20 viewport units, ~119x132dp here, a ~178dp diagonal.
  The plane and its exhaust are authored inside the same span so neither outgrows the circle.
-->
<animated-vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:aapt="http://schemas.android.com/aapt">

    <aapt:attr name="android:drawable">
        <vector
            android:width="288dp"
            android:height="288dp"
            android:viewportWidth="24"
            android:viewportHeight="24">
            <group
                android:name="craft"
                android:scaleX="0.55"
                android:scaleY="0.55"
                android:pivotX="12"
                android:pivotY="12">

                <!-- Exhaust sits in its own group so it can flare from the tail without
                     scaling the plane with it. The pivot is the tail notch (12,15), so the
                     flame stretches downward and stays anchored to the craft.

                     Both flame paths start at fillAlpha 0 — invisible through the draw-in
                     and the morph, since a bolt with a jet exhaust would make no sense.
                     Ignition brings them up only once the plane exists. -->
                <group
                    android:name="exhaust"
                    android:pivotX="12"
                    android:pivotY="15">
                    <!-- Outer plume. Curved rather than straight-sided: a polygon reads as a
                         cut gem, not fire — the belly has to bulge near the nozzle and taper
                         off. Only the morphing `bolt` path is constrained to line segments;
                         these are free to use curves.

                         Three gradient stops, not two. A straight saturated-orange-to-clear
                         ramp spends its whole midsection at partial alpha over a near-black
                         background, which composites to brown. Holding a bright stop through
                         the first third and dropping alpha late keeps the flame reading as
                         light rather than as a dark shape. -->
                    <path
                        android:name="flameOuter"
                        android:pathData="M12,15.5 C13.3,17.9 13.8,19.8 13.4,21.8 C13.1,23.6 12.6,25.2 12,26.4 C11.4,25.2 10.9,23.6 10.6,21.8 C10.2,19.8 10.7,17.9 12,15.5 Z"
                        android:fillAlpha="0">
                        <aapt:attr name="android:fillColor">
                            <gradient
                                android:type="linear"
                                android:startX="12"
                                android:startY="15.5"
                                android:endX="12"
                                android:endY="26.4">
                                <item android:offset="0" android:color="#FFFFD9A0" />
                                <item android:offset="0.3" android:color="#FFFF9A45" />
                                <item android:offset="0.62" android:color="#66FF6B35" />
                                <item android:offset="1" android:color="#00FF5E3A" />
                            </gradient>
                        </aapt:attr>
                    </path>
                    <!-- Inner core: shorter, narrower and white-hot at the nozzle, so the
                         plume above reads as the cooler edge of the same flame. -->
                    <path
                        android:name="flameInner"
                        android:pathData="M12,16.8 C12.8,18.5 13.1,19.9 12.9,21.3 C12.7,22.6 12.4,23.6 12,24.4 C11.6,23.6 11.3,22.6 11.1,21.3 C10.9,19.9 11.2,18.5 12,16.8 Z"
                        android:fillAlpha="0">
                        <aapt:attr name="android:fillColor">
                            <gradient
                                android:type="linear"
                                android:startX="12"
                                android:startY="16.8"
                                android:endX="12"
                                android:endY="24.4">
                                <item android:offset="0" android:color="#FFFFFFFF" />
                                <item android:offset="0.45" android:color="#FFFFD98A" />
                                <item android:offset="1" android:color="#00FFC46B" />
                            </gradient>
                        </aapt:attr>
                    </path>
                </group>

                <path
                    android:name="bolt"
                    android:pathData="M13,2 L3,14 L12,14 L11,22 L21,10 L12,10 Z"
                    android:strokeWidth="2.5"
                    android:strokeLineCap="round"
                    android:strokeLineJoin="round"
                    android:trimPathStart="0"
                    android:trimPathEnd="0">
                    <aapt:attr name="android:strokeColor">
                        <gradient
                            android:type="linear"
                            android:startX="0"
                            android:startY="0"
                            android:endX="24"
                            android:endY="24"
                            android:startColor="#7DD3FF"
                            android:endColor="#C084FC" />
                    </aapt:attr>
                </path>
            </group>
        </vector>
    </aapt:attr>

    <!-- Phase 1: draw the bolt in. -->
    <target android:name="bolt">
        <aapt:attr name="android:animation">
            <objectAnimator
                android:propertyName="trimPathEnd"
                android:valueFrom="0"
                android:valueTo="1"
                android:valueType="floatType"
                android:duration="360"
                android:interpolator="@android:interpolator/fast_out_slow_in" />
        </aapt:attr>
    </target>

    <!-- Phase 2: bolt unfolds into a paper plane, nose up and ready to climb. -->
    <target android:name="bolt">
        <aapt:attr name="android:animation">
            <objectAnimator
                android:propertyName="pathData"
                android:valueFrom="M13,2 L3,14 L12,14 L11,22 L21,10 L12,10 Z"
                android:valueTo="M12,2 L7.5,11 L3,20 L12,15 L21,20 L16.5,11 Z"
                android:valueType="pathType"
                android:startOffset="420"
                android:duration="360"
                android:interpolator="@android:interpolator/fast_out_slow_in" />
        </aapt:attr>
    </target>

    <!-- Phase 3: ignition. The plume catches first and the core follows a beat later, which
         is what makes it read as lighting rather than switching on. -->
    <target android:name="flameOuter">
        <aapt:attr name="android:animation">
            <objectAnimator
                android:propertyName="fillAlpha"
                android:valueFrom="0"
                android:valueTo="1"
                android:valueType="floatType"
                android:startOffset="780"
                android:duration="120"
                android:interpolator="@android:interpolator/decelerate_quad" />
        </aapt:attr>
    </target>
    <target android:name="flameInner">
        <aapt:attr name="android:animation">
            <objectAnimator
                android:propertyName="fillAlpha"
                android:valueFrom="0"
                android:valueTo="1"
                android:valueType="floatType"
                android:startOffset="840"
                android:duration="120"
                android:interpolator="@android:interpolator/decelerate_quad" />
        </aapt:attr>
    </target>

    <!-- Phase 4: the burn holds at power until the exit takes over.

         Flicker is on scaleY only. Animating the flame's alpha here instead would fight the
         ignition animators above, which own fillAlpha — two animators driving one property
         on one target is how you get a flame that snaps back to invisible mid-loop.

         The two layers pulse at different rates on purpose: matched rates read as one shape
         breathing, while offset rates read as combustion. Neither divides evenly into the
         other, so they drift in and out of phase instead of resyncing every few cycles. -->
    <target android:name="exhaust">
        <aapt:attr name="android:animation">
            <objectAnimator
                android:propertyName="scaleY"
                android:valueFrom="0.82"
                android:valueTo="1.18"
                android:valueType="floatType"
                android:startOffset="900"
                android:duration="170"
                android:repeatCount="-1"
                android:repeatMode="reverse"
                android:interpolator="@android:interpolator/accelerate_decelerate" />
        </aapt:attr>
    </target>
    <target android:name="exhaust">
        <aapt:attr name="android:animation">
            <objectAnimator
                android:propertyName="scaleX"
                android:valueFrom="0.94"
                android:valueTo="1.06"
                android:valueType="floatType"
                android:startOffset="900"
                android:duration="230"
                android:repeatCount="-1"
                android:repeatMode="reverse"
                android:interpolator="@android:interpolator/accelerate_decelerate" />
        </aapt:attr>
    </target>

    <!-- Phase 5: engine rumble — a small, fast vertical shudder through the whole craft.
         Deliberately tiny (~0.2 viewport units, a couple of dp on screen) and much faster
         than the old hover: at this amplitude and rate it reads as a held engine vibrating,
         whereas anything slower or wider reads as the plane floating, which is the effect
         this replaced. -->
    <target android:name="craft">
        <aapt:attr name="android:animation">
            <objectAnimator
                android:propertyName="translateY"
                android:valueFrom="0"
                android:valueTo="-0.2"
                android:valueType="floatType"
                android:startOffset="900"
                android:duration="90"
                android:repeatCount="-1"
                android:repeatMode="reverse"
                android:interpolator="@android:interpolator/linear" />
        </aapt:attr>
    </target>

</animated-vector>
